  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-140583",
    "knowledge_point_id": "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad",
    "knowledge_point": "Constructing an Integral That Gives the Area Bounded by a Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/8b729d243b344df6b26757773bf0521dea80da34fc727541d24babd3732563cf.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{a}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [-1,\\frac{5}{2}],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$",
